title The lap that died inside its own hot pass
prompt The baton -- Bakery's lane, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4145, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; the compass, the Lindy effect, and the grain all say one sentence under the sound, which is that the durable thing is the one that has already survived being used. Applied to my own dead-letter box it named the lap: the oldest proven work in this tree is work that has passed its controls and has not yet been sent, so landing it outranks building beside it.
think Opened on a clean tree and started a cold roster pass. It refused at once with run_verdict=run_in_flight -- a --hot runner, pid 2669490, started 05:49, parent gone, still holding construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d. The prior lap had died inside its own hot pass.
think The runner printed its own repair rather than a number: kill -TERM releases the lock through the EXIT trap, where SIGKILL bypasses the trap and leaves it for the next pass to reap. Ran verbatim; the lock cleared in under twenty seconds.
obs A DEAD LAP LEAVES TWO KINDS OF WRECKAGE AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS IN THE TREE. The round-open stashed the work, so the tree read clean and every tree-shaped guard was happy; what stood in the way was a process, and the process outlived the session that owned it. A guard reading the tree could not have seen this, and the runner's own lock check is what did.
think Read stash@{0} before applying it -- 24 files, 1,398 insertions -- and checked each new path against HEAD. All four instruments were genuinely unlanded: scope_rank, scope_match.sh, index_shelf_repair.sh, the rostered witness. So the lap was a landing rather than a rebuild.
obs THE DERIVED SPINE WORKED EXACTLY AS WRITTEN. The parked row was booked %538. git fetch xy then reds_spine_derive_scan --next answered 540: upstream had spent 538 on a peer's ratchet row and 539 on another while my lap sat in the box. The stamp 20260907.045528 identified the row throughout; only the view moved. Renumbered to %540, headline 539 -> 540, and rewrote the parked log to cite the stamp rather than a number the spine had yet to bind.
obs THE RECOVERED TOOL MET ITS OWN FAULT ON ITS FIRST DAY, TWICE. index_row_bound was red at the cold open with two faults on today's shelf: an exact-duplicate row, 20260907.023053 written twice by a rebase, and 20260907.023053 standing above 20260907.050258. index_shelf_repair.sh sorted the inversion and proved its output a permutation before writing; it refused the duplicate, which is right, and a hand dropped the identical copy under the one-log-one-row law.
obs THE REFUSAL IS CORRECT AND THE POPULATION IS TWO KINDS. Two rows sharing a stamp with different text is a judgment about which log the row describes, and the tool is right to refuse it. Two rows that are byte-identical is decidable -- drop one -- and it is what a rebase actually produces. Named on the card as one leg and one control rather than taken, since the tool was landing on the same commit.
obs ERRATUM, written after the send. The obs above stops at 540 because that is where the row stood when it was written. The send then took two more rebases and the number moved twice more: a peer published 540 through 544 while this lap ran, so the row went to 545, and a peer took 545 in the second rebase, so it settled at 546 -- folded to construction/archive/REDS-the-file-nobody-reads-is-the-file-that-drifts-rows-546.md, which is where it now stands. The stamp 20260907.045528 identified it through all four numbers, which is the law working rather than failing: four renumbers, zero citation sweeps, because nothing outside the ledger ever spelled the number.
obs A THIRD GUARD JOINED THE FLAKE FAMILY. fleet_watch read red inside the pass and GREEN on re-run of the same tree, joining amphora_carry_negative and standing_equipment. All three are controls that fork a real external service -- this one builds a real tmux session -- and all three fired under eight ships on eight cores. That is now a shape rather than a coincidence, and it is COPAL's open question with three instances.
think Dropped one path out of the stash on purpose: .claude/settings.json carried effortLevel max -> medium, which is this ship's own reasoning depth and belongs to no lap's work. Named here rather than landed silently.
